---
/*
 * "How to read this": what a closure permit means, how the dates, windows
 * and chips work (the rules in src/lib/street-closures/closures.js), data
 * quirks, and the source.
 */
import { DATASET_URL, DATASET_NAME, DATASET_ID, SOON_DAYS, LONG_DAYS } from '../../lib/street-closures/config.js';
import { date } from '../../lib/format.js';

interface Props {
  meta: { rowsUpdatedAt: string | null } | null;
}
const { meta } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="dash-section" aria-labelledby="scl-about-title">
  <h2 class="chart-title" id="scl-about-title">How to read this</h2>

  <p class="callout callout-heads-up">
    <strong>Heads up:</strong> a permit allows the block to be fully closed between its start and end dates, but crews
    may only work part of that time, and some kinds of work (like sidewalk permits) may only take a lane, the curb or the
    sidewalk. Check the signs on site before you plan around a closure.
  </p>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>What counts as a closure</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p>
        Each closure is one block of one street, between two cross streets, that a DOT construction permit lets
        someone close. DOT's street map sometimes splits a block into several pieces, each listed with its own copy of
        the permit; we put the pieces back together and count that permit once. A block can have several permits at
        once (a crane and a debris container, say), so we show one card per block with every permit on it. Searching
        for a street finds blocks on that street and blocks where it's one of the cross streets.
      </p>
      <p>
        A closure counts in a time window if it's in effect on any day of it. Both the start and end dates count, so a
        permit that ends today is still on the “Today” tab. “This week” runs through Sunday and “This month” through
        the last day of the month. All dates are New York time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>What the labels mean</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <ul class="caveats">
        <li><strong>Active now:</strong> today falls between the permit's start and end dates.</li>
        <li><strong>Starts soon:</strong> not yet in effect, and starts within {SOON_DAYS} days.</li>
        <li><strong>Ending soon:</strong> in effect, and ends within {SOON_DAYS} days.</li>
        <li><strong>Long-running:</strong> the closure lasts more than {LONG_DAYS} days in all. Back-to-back permits on the same block count as one closure.</li>
        <li><strong>Kind of work:</strong> DOT's permit types, in plain English and grouped for the bars. Some permit names mention a “protected” street: one repaved or rebuilt in the last few years, where the rules for digging it up are stricter. It's the same work, so we group it the same way.</li>
        <li><strong>Distance:</strong> from your spot to the nearest point of the block.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>How we cleaned the data</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p>
        Some permits appear more than once, word for word; we count each permit once (by its unique id). Permits whose
        end date has passed are left out. Street names are matched however they're written, so “W 8th St” finds
        “WEST 8 STREET”. Your location never leaves your browser except as a rounded spot (about a block) used to
        find nearby closures, and a typed address never goes in the page link.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <footer class="section-sources">
    <h3 class="sources-title">Where this comes from</h3>
    <p>
      <a href={DATASET_URL}>{DATASET_NAME}</a> on NYC Open Data (dataset {DATASET_ID}), published by the NYC Department
      of Transportation.
      {meta?.rowsUpdatedAt && <> Last updated {date(meta.rowsUpdatedAt)}. </>}
      DOT updates it about once a week; we check for changes every hour.
    </p>
    <p><a href={DATASET_URL}>Report a data problem</a> to NYC Open Data.</p>
  </footer>
</section>
